{#大道大商佛教与企业文化}
# Great Path, Great Business------Buddhism and Corporate Culture

------A Subforum Dialogue at the Fourth World Buddhist Forum, 2015

Master Jiqun

[I. What Is Success? [2](#一什么是成功)](#一什么是成功)

[II. Morality and Benefit [9](#二道德与利益)](#二道德与利益)

[III. Entrepreneurs' Views of Charity [13](#三企业家的慈善观)](#三企业家的慈善观)

[IV. Audience Q&A: Addressing Practical Concerns [21](#四观众互动解决问题)](#四观众互动解决问题)

Moderator: Mr. Yang Jinlin

Guests: Master Jiqun, Mr. Cao Dewang, Mr. Yang Zhao, Mr. Lee Kim Yew, and others

We live in an age dominated by commerce. Material civilization is highly developed, and accumulating wealth has already become the common measure of whether one succeeds. Yet many people have wealth but are unhappy, and their enterprises flourish yet they do not know their purpose. Meanwhile, the loss of social morality, ecological decline, and coldness in human relationships are all alarming. Where exactly has it gone wrong? I believe the key problem lies in society's standards of judgment.

Fortunately, many thoughtful people have recognized this problem. Guided by the wisdom of the Dharma, they have run businesses and gained rich experience in fostering healthy corporate development in today's society. We urgently need, therefore, to carry forward fine Eastern traditions and rebuild corporate culture. The “Buddhism and Corporate Culture Forum” will join entrepreneurs in exploring happiness, success, benefit, charity, and other topics from a Buddhist perspective, in the hope of rebuilding standards of success and exploring a path to happiness.

{#一什么是成功}
## I. What Is Success?

Yang Jinlin: I am moderating “Great Path, Great Business,” the New Media Forum at the Fourth World Buddhist Forum. It is an opportunity the forum has given me to serve, and an important and auspicious occasion for sharing. Today's theme is compelling and, I believe, will resonate widely. Let me first welcome our guests to the stage: Master Jiqun, Mr. Cao Dewang, Mr. Yang Zhao, and Mr. Lee Kim Yew.

Whatever our wealth or learning, we have come with one goal: to explore the relationship between Buddhism and running a business. We have four starting points: first, what is success; second, the relationship between wealth and benefit; third, what view of charity a successful entrepreneur should have; and fourth, what happiness we should pursue.

Our four speakers today include an eminent monastic and successful entrepreneurs who are also lay Buddhists. Each will offer a different perspective on today's subject.

In the more than thirty years since reform and opening up, we have become the world's second-largest economy and amassed the world's largest foreign-exchange reserves. At the same time, wealth has become society's measure of whether a person is successful.

Are these familiar standards true? Are they reasonable? We can see the price we have paid for development: environmental decline, worsening social mores, and a lack of trust between people. What message is this forum earnestly trying to convey to the world? Put differently, can the Dharma offer a new standard of judgment to this troubled world? Master Jiqun, please share your teaching.

Master Jiqun: It is a rare and auspicious occasion to explore some major issues in today's social development with several distinguished lay Buddhists who are entrepreneurs. Let us begin with what success is.

Why discuss this subject? Because how we view and pursue success is a barometer of social development. How we define “success” reflects our values. Whatever we consider valuable in life, acquiring it means success, and the reverse is also true.

Today, when material civilization is stronger than ever, people often measure success by wealth. Familiar rankings such as the Forbes list make that standard concrete and visible. Across the media, the richest person in the world or in China, in a province or city, even in a county or village, appears again and again as rankings constantly change. It has become a serial drama avidly followed and discussed by the whole nation.

When all society takes money as the mark of success, “maximizing profit” naturally comes first. Such values further inflame greed, until it blinds people and they will stop at nothing for profit. I think we have all seen the resulting moral decline, ecological damage, lawbreaking, and other problems. Worse still, integrity faces an unprecedented crisis------we do not know what to trust, nor do we dare trust anything.

Clearly, a flawed understanding of success poses a major danger to social development. We urgently need to reconsider the value of success and define what it means; this is also crucial to China's healthy development in the future. It concerns not only each person's vital interests but the stability and harmony of society as a whole.

For millennia, Chinese values came mainly from traditional Chinese culture. The Confucian “three forms of immortality,” for instance, were once mainstream values that influenced countless people. “First, establish virtue” made moral achievement and sage-like character the highest aim. “Next, establish merit” meant building a worthy career and benefiting one's community. “Next, establish words” meant influencing society and educating people through writing. Correspondingly, there was “cultivate oneself, regulate the family, govern the state, and bring peace to all under heaven”: begin by perfecting one's moral character, then manage family relationships well, govern the country, benefit society, and bring peace and well-being to the world.

Under the influence of these values, people in ancient times took as their life's aim: “Establish a heart for Heaven and Earth; establish a destiny for the people; carry forward the lost learning of past sages; and bring lasting peace to all generations.” Rather than merely a noble spiritual pursuit, this was how they understood and practiced success.

From a Buddhist perspective, values rest on an understanding of life. Where does life's value lie? In perfecting wisdom and compassion. We perfect wisdom by studying the scriptures and teachings, awakening our innate awareness, and thus ending delusion and affliction to become awakened beings; this emphasizes benefiting oneself. We perfect compassion by extending concern from ourselves to others, dedicating this profound heart to worlds as numerous as dust, and centering sentient beings rather than ourselves; this emphasizes benefiting others. Buddhist success, then, is not only personal accomplishment but the benefit of all. We can even say that benefiting oneself serves the purpose of benefiting others, and awakening oneself ultimately serves to awaken others. Hence the saying, “For the benefit of sentient beings, may I attain Buddhahood.”

This definition of success depends on understanding the truth of life. Only on that basis can we view success from a higher perspective rather than confining ourselves to immediate gain and loss.

Yang Jinlin: Thank you, Master Jiqun. Our other three guests are renowned entrepreneurs and devout lay Buddhists who support the Dharma. They are neither the children of officials nor heirs to wealth. Each started from nothing and, through personal effort, climbed step by step to the height of success. They continue to create value for society today. Mr. Yang Zhao, please tell us what success means to you.

Yang Zhao: As an entrepreneur, I divide my view of success into a first half and a second half. The first is success in one's career; the second, success in life. Career success requires only fame, profit, and social standing. What, then, defines success in life? Good health, a happy family, and whether you are happy in life.

Society now makes the mistake of equating career success with success in life. Yet many people see someone who appears successful and think, “He is not my role model.” Why? Though rich and famous, he has an unhappy family and does not enjoy life. Clearly, a career is not the same as a life, and career success is not success in life. So beyond pursuing career success, in the second half we must pursue success in life.

Yang Jinlin: First half, second half------it sounds like a football match. Mr. Cao, do you agree with this way of playing?

Cao Dewang: I believe they are all right, but success is a broad subject. As the saying goes, the same rice feeds a hundred kinds of people. Across all walks of life, whose success are we judging? I think we should assess the success of sentient beings. What does success mean for sentient beings? Obeying laws and regulations, having a strong body and a happy, complete family, and living freely and at ease------that is success.

Master Jiqun just mentioned the restlessness of Chinese people and the Forbes list. Of course he was not targeting me. I am an old hand at appearing on Forbes; they put me on the list all the time. I once asked them why they paid so much attention to these Chinese people. They said China's reform and opening up, and its rapid annual GDP growth, had caught the attention of the entire Western world. First, people wanted to know how China had done it and whether it was real. Second, they wanted to know whether they had a chance to take part. Third, if they did have a chance, they wanted to know whom to approach in China. So the ranking really provides information, while the ranking itself is illusory.

Why? It forms an intermediate link in the virtual economy. In a virtual economy, once you put money into an investment, whether in a real business or in stocks and futures, it becomes insubstantial. One sudden event, whatever it may be, can wipe out your property overnight. If you want to get things done, keep your money safely in the bank and do not move it. That is real; move it, and it becomes virtual. What stands at the front line of investment is often insubstantial. Quite a few people who once appeared on rich lists eventually ended up in an embarrassing predicament. So we should not call them restless. We can only say that we do not understand this field well enough and need to improve how we assess these people.

People recently said someone had become rich, when in fact his company's debt was several times his personal assets. Forbes itself is therefore insubstantial. It is playing a game, and it also serves the publicity needs of national reform and opening up. Do not take it too seriously.

Yang Jinlin: Mr. Cao has given us another perspective. Mr. Lee Kim Yew of Malaysia has been a good friend of mine for many years. Please tell us how you understand success.

Lee Kim Yew: For me, success is always a process and is hard to define. I very much agree with Mr. Yang's first and second halves of life. Succeeding at one thing is easy, but pursuing success in life is a profound undertaking. I think anyone who wishes to succeed needs several qualities: first self-confidence, then diligence, and third, dedication and a willingness to learn humbly. I feel very fortunate that I began studying Buddhism at forty. We can never finish learning from the great wisdom of our fundamental teacher, Shakyamuni Buddha.

Today we have the chance to learn in the presence of a great Dharma teacher like Master Jiqun, as well as Lay Buddhists Yang and Cao, who also have much to teach us. Learning with humility takes time; only sustained effort will bear fruit. This is especially important for young people today.

Yang Jinlin: These speakers make me feel truly unsuccessful. I am still starting businesses at sixty, with neither a first half nor a second. Only those who have finished playing the first half can leisurely discuss life, health, and happiness. Without a certain material foundation, empty or extravagant talk of success is meaningless.

But Master Jiqun has also given us something to think about. The clash between Western material standards and Eastern spiritual standards can cause sentient beings much distress------and I may be the most troubled of all. That is why I came today not merely to moderate, but to gain some wisdom.

Speaking of success, honestly, wealth now determines one's standing in the world. Mr. Cao has exposed the truth for us: do not believe rich lists; they are for publicity. Yet many people really do leap into them. Chasing empty fame and gain is one of society's present ills. Its emptiness, compounded by a lack of faith, has produced so much disorder.

Why hold a Buddhist forum like this? Why do monastics take such pains to persuade us? Whether in the first half or the second, is putting profit above all else right or wrong? Mr. Yang------my fellow Yang------what do you think?

Yang Zhao: As we just said, wealth has two parts: material wealth and spiritual wealth. We now make the mistake of thinking material wealth gives us everything. I have met many major entrepreneurs in Hong Kong, but money cannot buy everything. It cannot buy health, a happy family, or happiness in life.

Success in life therefore requires spiritual wealth. Do not misdiagnose the problem; prescribe the right remedy. Only when we have both material wealth------money, fame, and social standing------and spiritual wealth can life be successful. Fame, profit, and social standing alone amount only to career success, not success in life. Career success thus has three measures: wealth, fame, and status. Success in life adds three more: physical health, family happiness, and joy in living.

Yang Jinlin: Honestly, not many of us here today may meet all six measures. Otherwise, why would people be standing to hear our forum? Clearly, everyone has come for a prescription. Sentient beings are equal, but we are not necessarily equal in our understanding of life. That equality is relative. Master Jiqun, as a monastic, what do you think of the many standards Mr. Yang has set? Are they hard to meet?

Master Jiqun: What Lay Buddhist Yang says does reflect reality. Many entrepreneurs pursue these two levels. But separating career success from success as a human being can still easily cause problems.

If we detach our career from our goal as human beings and pursue career success alone, we may treat our career as everything. For our work, we ruin our health, disrupt our families, and even damage our state of mind. This happens everywhere. So I believe that pursuing career success should rest on being a decent human being.

This does not mean everyone who pursues a career will encounter these problems. If someone already has moral boundaries and principles for dealing with the world, even without deliberately pursuing success as a person, they will bring the proper character to their work, make sound choices, and know what to do and what to refuse. Conversely, without those qualities, someone who single-mindedly pursues business success can easily act recklessly and cause all kinds of problems.

Yang Jinlin: In the first half, you make money with all your might. Only after earning enough do you realize, “Oh dear, I have failed as a human being,” and begin giving back to society. Is that a mindset of atonement or correction? Mr. Cao, please continue to teach us.

Cao Dewang: At this point, I can proudly say that entrepreneurs can learn from me. I succeeded by simplifying complex problems. I classify the *Four Books and Five Classics*, the *Chinese Buddhist Canon*, and so forth as education. That is cause, not result. Education has countless strands, but I have distilled them into three: faith, aspiration, and practice.

That is how I have conducted myself from the day I started my business until now. My reputation is strong in China and even stronger in America and Europe, where people respect me greatly. Why? In doing business with them, I learned to respect others. I firmly believe in the dignity of the law and the power of public opinion. I firmly believe that my success depends on the support of many customers and on employees who shed blood and sweat and work with unwavering loyalty. I firmly believe that the world is full of truth, goodness, and beauty. I also believe that I must take every step carefully and contend with hidden arrows.

Through diligent study, I avoid all kinds of risk. Everyone now finds business difficult, yet this year we are still growing rapidly over last year. What lesson have I drawn? Simplify complex problems. First comes faith.

Second, I have a broad vision. I made a vow to make a piece of glass for the Chinese people, one that could embody our national standard, engage the world, and display both Chinese wisdom and the success of reform and opening up. I now produce automotive glass for the world. To put it modestly, mine is the second-largest company, because BMW, Mercedes-Benz, Audi, Volkswagen, General Motors, and Honda are all my customers.

If you truly want success, begin with small things. Everything that happens in every day and every minute is a brick in the building of your life. Before laying each brick, ask whether it belongs there. Once laid crooked, it cannot later be removed. You may say, “I am only building this high, so it is no problem.” But if the bricks are laid well, when the building one day reaches a hundred stories, it will not collapse because one brick was wrongly placed.

If your life contains a disreputable episode, once you rise high and the pressure mounts, that weak point cannot bear it------as the saying goes, it is cold at the top. Someone may say, “So-and-so once committed this offense or cheated at that.” Then you are finished; down you go. Earning any amount of money is easy, in my view. Living an entire life that leaves others no grounds to accuse you is much harder. As Chairman Mao Zedong said, doing one good deed is easy; what is hard is doing good deeds all your life.

I hope entrepreneurs will take this as encouragement. It too comes down to faith------you must cultivate your own faith until it is clear. If I succeed, I believe the Buddha has given me this blessing. If I fail, I accept my fate; I was destined to take this step. I also believe in a future life: when this life is done, I will return in the next.

Yang Jinlin: Openhearted. Only a richly lived life yields such insight. Malaysia successfully hosted both APEC and 10+3, and Mr. Lee Kim Yew and his group handled the arrangements. An entrepreneur who can organize such events for his country surely counts as successful. How do you interpret Mr. Cao's view? He gives me the sense that sincerity and kindness are rewarded. Even if the reward has not come in this life, never mind; that is fate, and I have another life. It is a wonderfully clear-eyed view of life. What do you think?

Lee Kim Yew: When Mr. Cao says entrepreneurs can learn from him, he is not being conceited. He feels he has strong positive energy to offer young people. This is what I just said: success requires self-confidence.

Speaking honestly, if we discuss “success” today, a business cannot avoid talking about making money. In my experience, a merchant must create profit; if you cannot, you are not a merchant. More important, however, is to “think of righteousness at the sight of gain.” As the saying goes, “A gentleman values wealth but acquires it by the proper way.” There is nothing wrong with making a profit. Mr. Cao, for example, turns sand into glass and then into automotive windshields: a gentleman benefits things through righteousness. The *Book of Changes* says, “Benefit is the harmony of righteousness.” As long as you build your enterprise with a spirit of sharing, you can share not only your technological achievements but also the wealth you create. This accords with the conduct of a Buddhist practitioner. I believe Mr. Cao has truly lived out what he described.

The moderator just mentioned the conferences I organized in Malaysia. I have not only organized major conferences but supported many social and cultural activities. I love the four words “origin, flourishing, benefit, and constancy.” I recite them as though reciting “Buddha Amitabha.” Origin is the chief of goodness; whatever we do should begin with a wholesome intention. Flourishing is wisdom, and our discussion here is flourishing. Thus “benefiting things is enough to harmonize righteousness.” I saw all of this in what Mr. Cao just said.

Yang Jinlin: The forum and dialogue are gathering momentum. Mr. Yang Zhao introduced this topic by analyzing success in terms of a first and second half, even quantifying the standard------the Yang Method of Standards. Yet we can see that our three speakers approach the subject from different angles. I suggest we seek common ground while respecting differences.

Mr. Yang Zhao, I know you still have many insights to share. Your view of happiness has left me so dejected that I wonder whether there is any point in staying alive and continuing the struggle. Before you speak, however, I would like Dharma Master Benru of Jingye Temple, the ancestral seat of the Vinaya school on Mount Zhongnan, to respond from the floor. If this forum benefits everyone, everyone should take part.

Dharma Master Benru: Thank you all, and thank you, Mr. Yang Jinlin, for giving me a chance to speak. As I receive your wisdom, I have also been wondering: what is the greatest success for a monastic? My answer is to help Bodhisattva Ksitigarbha become a Buddha soon! My teacher, Venerable Miaozhan, once told me that life calls for three kinds of health: first physical health, second mental health, and third healthy wisdom. With these, life will certainly be splendid, and we can help Bodhisattva Ksitigarbha and all sentient beings become Buddhas. This is what “Great Path, Great Business” means for every Buddhist practitioner.

{#二道德与利益}
## II. Morality and Benefit

Yang Jinlin: Mr. Yang Zhao, I asked Dharma Master Benru to give you an opening. I think you should continue the discussion.

Yang Zhao: We were just discussing morality. How should we view wealth from a moral perspective? Different people see it differently. The Confucian view is, “Do not impose on others what you would not want for yourself.” In other words, put yourself in another person's shoes and see things from their perspective------what we call empathy. If we apply the same principle and look at the issue from common ground, we can agree on “Do not impose on others what you would not want for yourself.”

Buddhists also have moral standards. As a Buddhist, I uphold the Five Precepts and Ten Wholesome Deeds, practice the Six Perfections, and learn the Four Immeasurables of loving-kindness, compassion, empathetic joy, and equanimity. These are my standards for living and acting.

Yang Jinlin: Two months ago, I went to the North Pole. On the same ship, Li Yinhe spoke with me about three views of life: first, benefiting others; second, benefiting oneself; and third, benefiting oneself a little while helping others and helping others a little while benefiting oneself. These are three ways to live. After hearing this, I felt I had wasted sixty years. Was I benefiting others, benefiting myself, or somewhere in between? I could not work out what I was living for.

When it comes to benefit, merchants and entrepreneurs cannot liberate all sentient beings unless they seek profit. Fuyao Glass, for example, could not support its ten or twenty thousand workers because expenses must be paid. The key is whether our starting point is benefiting others or ourselves. Is it really so absolute, Mr. Cao?

Cao Dewang: When it comes to wealth, I do not think any amount deserves envy or must be pursued, but we absolutely cannot do without it. When I was so poor I could not afford a pack of cigarettes, even seeing a one-cent coin excited me. Now that I have money, I find it useless; enough to spend is enough, because money changes. Governments everywhere now promote loose monetary policies. One misstep, and the money in your drawer immediately loses value. The same is true of stocks: you may lose money or strike it rich. These are very real issues. Only one thing must remain constant: improving one's overall capabilities and qualities. That is the greatest wealth.

Confucianism has a saying: “I do not seek storehouses of gold and jade; I only wish that every descendant be worthy.” What we should leave our children is wisdom and ability. If you have competence, reputation, credibility, and a healthy body, you have the greatest wealth. Benefit takes countless forms. Being invited to sit here in the center while you sit below also benefits me greatly, because everyone wants this seat. The ancients spoke of “balancing righteousness and benefit.” When you occupy a position, fulfill its duties; when you hold a position, honor your word.

As mentioned earlier, China does not distinguish merchants from entrepreneurs. It is like Westerners saying China has no philosophy while Chinese people reply, “No, we do.” Take the lines, “Who first saw the moon above the riverbank? In what year did the river moon first shine upon a person?” Nan Huai-Chin said that is philosophy. How could China have none?

Why do Westerners say China has no entrepreneurs? How do they define one? During Western industrialization, entrepreneurs received very high honors. An entrepreneur, they believed, must first have courage: courage to take responsibility, challenge limits, challenge the future, challenge the world, and challenge all under heaven. Accomplish that and you have mettle. If Volkswagen's deception is confirmed this time, it will recall more than 13 million cars. They say the cost is 18 billion U.S. dollars------unimaginable, isn't it? So an entrepreneur first needs the courage to take responsibility.

Small traders earn modest sums to support their families. A true entrepreneur, however, must accept responsibility, and has ambitions beyond ordinary business. Entrepreneurs compete under uniform standards, including standards for quality, accounting, and conduct. Through their own effort, they continually improve production equipment, product quality, and efficiency while reducing costs.

Who worries about these things? The people do, society does, and the country does. An entrepreneur's ambition should therefore be this: the country grows stronger because you exist, society advances because you exist, and the people prosper because you exist.

At the same time, your efforts move everyone to strive by these standards------that is the difference between an entrepreneur and a merchant. We learned from the West: first from the Soviet Union, then from America. America taught us what it was then experimenting with: “deindustrialization” and the virtual economy. Consequently, all we learned was the virtual economy. The stock market has opened a host of boards------the Third Board, Fourth Board, and Growth Enterprise Market. I ask them: do you want to build a good company? Are you making products or selling shares? What is your goal?------That is the problem. You call these people bad, but do not criticize them; whatever exists has its reasons. After opening up, this is what we learned from abroad. What can you do?

Yang Jinlin: Great Business has had plenty to say; the Great Path should speak too. Buddhism and corporate culture have gradually found a path, at least a path for reflection. Whether we are discussing views of wealth or benefiting oneself and others, Master Jiqun, please offer us some guidance.

Master Jiqun: When we discuss wealth, morality, and the debate between righteousness and benefit, what do we need to examine? Why does society as a whole now lack a sense of morality? We cannot simply condemn what we see; we also need to ask what causes it.

This social situation has something to do with both our tradition and our current education. The Confucian “debate between righteousness and benefit,” for example, sets righteousness and benefit somewhat in opposition. It suggests that a moral person should not discuss benefit and, conversely, that discussing benefit somehow shows disregard for morality.

Traditional Chinese morality also rested on the patriarchal clan system. That is, morality served the family's needs, then society's needs, while personal needs came last. People therefore often felt that morality was a form of social conduct with little relevance to themselves. Once the family's and society's power to constrain them weakened, morality faded too. For a time, people even issued the brazen challenge: “How much is morality per pound?” Its unspoken message was this: if you cannot constrain me, why should I behave myself and follow your rules?

Under the influence of materialist education, the basis for morality grew even weaker. A saying holds that “a thoroughgoing materialist fears nothing.” What do such people not fear? Karmic cause and effect. If someone cares only about immediate, visible advantage and disadvantage and takes no longer view, following morality becomes hollow. So we must find the root of moral decline. Only then can we offer effective solutions.

I believe moral conduct depends on wisdom. Without wisdom, moral rules become mechanical------just a list of things to do and not do. That raises a question: why should I follow them? Acting morally often seems to put us at a disadvantage; it asks us to give something up and exercise restraint. If we lose out, why do it? In the past, people may have complied because of ritual norms, law, or public opinion. But today, when personal freedom is prized, people may care about none of these. Why should they still live morally?

We therefore need to understand morality's value to life itself. Through awakening, the Buddha told us that life is an endless accumulation. Our present state of life comes from our past actions, speech, and thoughts, also called the three karmas of body, speech, and mind. These create life's many different states. Wholesome actions build wholesome accumulations in life, fostering a healthy state of mind, character, and quality of life. Conversely, unwholesome actions build harmful accumulations and lead life downward.

Clearly, when we practice morality, we ourselves are its greatest beneficiaries; only then do others benefit. Conversely, we become the first victims of our own immoral conduct and then harm others. With this understanding, people will follow morality consciously, not for anyone else but to take responsibility for their own lives and futures. Everyone wants a bright future, a healthy state of mind, and noble character.

If we can understand morality's value at this level, everyone will willingly choose to live morally, just as we willingly work for a better life.

From society's perspective, following morality benefits not only oneself but others, or at least does not harm them. In this sense, the law is morality's minimum. When everyone obeys the law, society is secure. Consider Buddhist precepts, even the most basic Five Precepts: not killing, stealing, engaging in sexual misconduct, lying, or consuming intoxicants. Simply observing them both restrains us and gives others a sense of safety. Imagine how desirable a society without harm, theft, betrayal, or deceit would be.

Buddhism also has a nobler morality, the morality of buddhas and bodhisattvas------selfless benefit for others, and great loving-kindness and compassion. Seeing sentient beings as more important than oneself, one stands ready to help them leave suffering and find happiness. Practicing such morality brings comfort and hope to so many.

The distinguished lay Buddhists here practice precisely this. Guided by faith in the Dharma, they run their businesses and conduct themselves in the world. That is one important reason for their success. We should not separate morality sharply from benefit.

{#三企业家的慈善观}
## III. Entrepreneurs' Views of Charity

Yang Jinlin: Morality is not abstract but concrete. At different stages in life, and especially now, our three entrepreneurs have worked hard to practice it, giving wealth, Dharma, and freedom from fear. Mr. Yang, you must have many thoughts on benefiting others and yourself to share, along with your personal practice of charity.

Yang Zhao: We just spoke about views of wealth, or the purpose of life. How should we live? How should we act? Where is the road? Beneath our feet.

First, we must eat. How do we do that? We must obey the law; we cannot rob others. That is the minimum standard. Second comes the higher standard of conscience. Ask yourself whether your conscience is clear: have you wronged your customers, your family, society, the country, or humanity?

Each night, if I find that my conscience is clear about the day, I sleep very well. If instead I find that I did one or two things poorly------perhaps I was too serious, too impatient, or lost my temper again------what should I do? I must repent: “Yang Zhao, you must keep trying tomorrow.”

“Each day I examine myself on three counts.” We must reflect on and examine our own conduct. By learning from what we find and drawing lessons from it, we can keep improving.

Yang Jinlin: Kim Yew, please share your thoughts too.

Lee Kim Yew: I am sixty this year, so I count as an old man. When I was forty, China had only just begun reform and opening up. Much of what nourished me came from Taiwan. Taiwan once gave me an “Overseas Youth Entrepreneurship Award,” my first award for entrepreneurship. Afterward, many people called me an entrepreneur. In fact, the Chinese title *qiyejia*, “entrepreneur,” troubled me for years because its final character, *jia*, is a weighty title. The same character appears in the Chinese words for painter and pianist, yet not everyone who paints becomes a painter, and not everyone who plays the piano becomes a pianist.

So at the time, I established a young entrepreneurs' association in Malaysia, believing it could help society and young people. My purpose was simple: when one person starts a business, it creates at least some jobs. Someone who builds a great enterprise like Mr. Cao creates many jobs.

A business must develop gradually. You cannot register a company today and make your fortune tomorrow. Yet many people now seek quick success and instant gain. This is a widespread social problem. So I believe young people today should first be honest and steady “businesspeople.”

Consider the three Chinese characters for “businessperson,” *sheng-yi-ren*. I looked up *sheng*, “to produce.” The *Great Learning* says: “There is a great way to produce wealth: let many produce it and few consume it; let its producers work quickly and its users spend slowly; then wealth will always be sufficient.” Laozi's *Daodejing* also says: “Give birth but do not possess; act but do not presume on your ability; accomplish your work but claim no credit. Precisely because you claim no credit, your achievement endures.” Those of us who run businesses must therefore have productivity and creativity.

The second character, *yi*, “intention,” is even more important. When running a business, our intention must be right. We can draw much nourishment here from Confucianism, Buddhism, and Daoism. The *Diamond Sutra* often asks, “What do you think?”------literally, “What is your intention?” If you manufacture products for babies to eat or drink, how could you make anything with bad intentions? How could you start a business, make money, and then leave society harmed? Many of today's environmental problems likewise arise from wrong intentions.

The third character is *ren*, “person,” the most important of all. Its two strokes stand for truth and uprightness; we must do what a person should. How should we live? As we just said, this is a profound field of study: we need filial devotion, fraternal affection, loyalty, and trustworthiness. Think about it. If you are not filial to your parents, show no affection to your siblings, and constantly quarrel at home, then however successful your career outside may be, your life will still have problems. Why does Confucianism value filial devotion, fraternal affection, loyalty, and trustworthiness so highly? Because they concern how we live as human beings.

Mr. Cao just said that an “entrepreneur” differs from a merchant, and I strongly agree. I think the three characters of “businessperson” encompass that distinction. When others call you an *entrepreneur*, you should ask yourself: am I worthy of that *jia*?

I was born in Malaysia and began with one hundred dollars. But I am a little greedy: I want my family, especially my children, to become people of wealth and honor. Why? Confucianism says, “Wealth and rank gained unrighteously are to me like drifting clouds.” Confucius did not tell us to reject wealth and honor. He warned us not to acquire wealth unjustly.

If we study Buddhism, Confucianism, or Daoism while living in utter poverty, with people unable to make a living, how can we study? In fact, studying any of these traditions helps us become people of wealth and honor. Learn a little and you gain a little wealth; learn to an intermediate level and you gain more; learn deeply and you gain the most.

So I think we should encourage young people today to become people of wealth and honor. But there are three conditions: be wealthy and love learning, be wealthy and love propriety, and be wealthy and love goodness. As for honor, we just heard, “Do not impose on others what you would not want for yourself.” There is also, “Seek to establish others as you would establish yourself.” Everyone meets many benefactors in life. Sometimes our parents are benefactors; sometimes teachers guide and advance us. Once successful, you too should become a benefactor to others: honor yourself and honor others.

To be a “businessperson and a person of wealth and honor,” we also need “morality.” In Chinese, *de*, “virtue,” means *de*, “gain”; the two sound alike------only with the Way can we gain. If you want great wealth, many customers, and all manner of other things, you must build them on morality. Like a house, you need a sound foundation. Practice morality well and you will “gain.” But then the question arises: what is the Way?

Yang Jinlin: Thank you for sharing, Kim Yew. Today's topic is truly meaningful. At a Buddhist forum, we have set aside time to discuss businesspeople and people of wealth and honor. To be wealthy and love learning, propriety, and goodness: wealth is the material foundation, while what one loves is spiritual wealth. I would also like Dharma Master Daoyuan to share his thoughts.

Dharma Master Daoyuan: Thank you, Mr. Yang, and thank you all for the opportunity to speak. I will share a few thoughts from the perspective of happiness. Before ordaining, I had a good job and good income. I was content and felt happy. After I ordained under my teacher, Venerable Huitong, I practiced in a thatched hut. He did not let us use mobile phones or even electricity, and I felt happy there too.

Later, at age eighty-three, the venerable made a vow to restore Yangqi Putong Temple, an ancestral Chan temple. He came to ask me to take full charge. I told him, “If I had wanted to be an abbot, I would not have come to you to ordain.” He replied, “You must form the aspiration. When you establish a practice center and care for everyone in their practice, you will discover that you too are happy.” So I went to Yangqi Putong Temple and took charge of its reconstruction. More than forty Dharma masters now practice there, and I feel very happy.

Perhaps this is the move from personal happiness to the happiness of benefiting others. Buddhism has a saying: “The Yangqi lamp shines through the ages.” At this point, I must thank Mr. Yang Zhao. Through my relationship with him, the temple established the “Heart-Lamp Aid Program.” Mr. Yang said, “First light the lamp in our own hearts, then help those in need.” Now, in Pingxiang, where the temple is located, Mr. Yang provides funds for every orphan who meets the criteria, and the temple administers the aid. Nearly a thousand people have received help. While we were helping these children and elderly people, they said, “Thank you, Master. No one has helped us like this in decades.” I too felt happy while helping them. But the happiness of benefiting others needs a material foundation. Without Mr. Yang's support, I could not help others.

Yang Jinlin: So many distinguished people have gathered here today, and this brings us to another topic. Charitable organizations now abound, and successful entrepreneurs pay more attention to charity. Mr. Cao Dewang, Mr. Yang Zhao, and Mr. Lee Kim Yew have given wealth and Dharma and personally lived these ideals for decades. We are deeply grateful. In one sense, charity has become widespread. Yet charity can sometimes cause distress. Many people do good only to face all kinds of criticism. They become conflicted and unhappy. Why? Master Jiqun, are our motives impure, or does society misunderstand charity?

Master Jiqun: These distinguished lay Buddhists all have extensive experience with charity. Today, as the gap between rich and poor grows, charity can ease tensions between them to some degree and is vital to society's healthy development. Yet many people do indeed feel conflicted when doing charity. Why?

I once spoke at a charity forum held by Fudan University called “Give Love Greater Power.” The audience consisted of charitable workers and caring people from across the country. The forum was held because many had encountered difficulties in their charitable work. That prompted me to reflect on the phenomenon.

I think it mainly involves two questions. First, what is charity? Can a donation alone be called charity? Many circumstances may lie behind a donation: it may come from love, from pressure exerted by public opinion, or from some other motive. Equating donation with charity while ignoring its motive is problematic. Charity must begin with love and compassion. Only actions grounded in them deserve to be called charity. In this sense, charity can also be called “the morality of compassion.”

We can see that both Buddhism and Christianity have charitable traditions and actively support public causes. Such action is guided by the compassion Buddhism advocates and the universal love Christianity teaches. Only when action rests on these ideas can it become genuine charity. Remove compassion and universal love, and we may help others with worldly thoughts or expectations. Once those expectations go unanswered, we will in fact feel distressed and suffer.

This leads to another question: what is charity's core value? Sometimes we may hope charitable work will bring us peace and ease, and society's respect and recognition. Such hopes are reasonable, but they are not charity's core value.

Charity's core value lies first in growing our inner compassion and elevating our character. Someone who practices charity should become ever more tolerant and loving. In society today, people often feel nothing for one another; there is alienation and indifference. Worse, society is filled with inexplicable hostility. As compassion grows through charitable work, it can dispel not only indifference, estrangement, and anger but also spread compassion and dissolve hostility. This is charity's true value for us. External recognition, praise, and respect come naturally, but they are merely by-products.

Yang Jinlin: Today's discussion has reached the heart of the matter. Mr. Cao is an extraordinary donor to my alma mater, Xiamen University; that too is great love. Mr. Yang Zhao and Mr. Lee Kim Yew have also accumulated so much merit and earned a place in Chinese communities. In this new era, the whole country is pursuing cultural renewal. The vision is inspiring, but the road lies beneath our feet------how should we take each step?

As we spread the Dharma and each entrepreneur puts these ideas into practice, how do we define success? The same rice feeds a hundred kinds of people; each person interprets it from a different angle. How do we form a relatively objective view of wealth, become a businessperson, and become a person of wealth and honor?

At the least, we must uphold the dignity of individuals, groups, and society. Without dignity, how can we give wealth, Dharma, and freedom from fear? Without compassion, how can we possibly have a sound view of happiness?

Speaking of charity, Mr. Cao Dewang, Mr. Yang Zhao, and Mr. Lee Kim Yew all practice it. They are happy and unconflicted, yet account for every cent, because only then can they give in the right way. Let us not discuss the hardest moments in each person's life; those have passed. Let us discuss the happiest moment. Was it sharing wealth, sharing happiness, sharing family joy, or sharing through charitable work? What was your happiest moment?

Mr. Yang Zhao has helped nearly a thousand orphans. What do all those children call you------Papa Yang?

Yang Zhao: I have several standards for happiness. First, contentment brings lasting joy. Contentment means that, by your own standards, you have enough food and clothing, your family's livelihood is secure, and you have enough wealth to help others. Suppose you support a thousand orphans and then realize, “Oh dear, I can support them this year but not next.” That easily causes distress. But if you have enough wealth, a thousand poses no problem, nor does ten thousand. How joyful would that be? So the first key to happiness is the lasting joy of contentment.

Second, find joy in helping others. Try it and you will discover that helping others is truly joyful.

Third, find joy within yourself. Do not make yourself miserable in trying to make others happy. How can you make yourself happy? Learn to find joy within. A cup of tea can bring joy; watching television can bring joy; shopping can bring joy too.

With all three------lasting joy from contentment, joy in helping others, and joy found within------life will be happy.

Yang Jinlin: Mr. Cao, it seems you have your own interpretation.

Cao Dewang: I can share a few things about charity. Where did my wealth come from? Back when I pulled a handcart, I picked up a straw hat by the roadside and put it on my head. Somehow I made a fortune. So I donated half of it, almost seven billion yuan. I have donated more than anyone else in China.

The Chinese are certainly better at charity than Westerners and have practiced it longer. Confucianism and Daoism both speak of charity. In what way is Buddhism superior? Buddhism teaches us to hold compassion in our hearts and regard sentient beings as equal. As Master Jiqun just said, first we must cultivate a compassionate heart. Compassion gives rise to charity. The purpose of charity is therefore clear------to firmly cultivate our faith in goodness and our powerful inner strength------that is why we do it.

All sentient beings are equal: not only you, me, and others, but all creatures born from eggs, wombs, and moisture. Even the great mountains lie within the scope of our protection. So no industry should act recklessly, boring a hole here or digging a ditch there. Mountains and rivers are alive and should be protected. If we destroy nature and our own living environment, what charity can we still do?

My understanding of charity therefore begins with humility, honesty, and obedience to the law. Whether running a company or factory, do not pollute the environment. Otherwise, how many people will lose everything paying for medical treatment?

Compassion in the heart and equality among sentient beings are thoroughly sound principles. China has a long history of charity; it began earlier, and its motives and goals were very clear. Why do we now lag behind developed Europe and America? When I made donations, I encountered income tax, so I looked into American practice. I found that America only began legislating on charity around the end of World War II, some seventy or eighty years ago. Before that, the government opposed it and ordinary people denounced it as fake. China is now also undergoing a charitable transition. Once the economy reaches a certain stage, it faces another issue; traditional Confucian charity alone is no longer enough.

On one side is philosophical understanding. On the other, the government should use incentives to encourage the wealthy to contribute money and take part in social redistribution. At one time in history, rich people were arrested and their land was divided up. That led to trouble.

Entrepreneurs must also recognize that they should develop together with the country. You did not become rich solely through your own ability. During the country's transition, the broom happened to sweep your way. Without that, you would still be who you were before. You must see this relationship. Developed countries now use pressure to make the wealthy participate in charity. That pressure takes the form of estate, income, and interest taxes. Will you hand over the money or not?

Why do we practice charity? To promote and sustain social stability. We should therefore examine how such a vast country can seek common ground while respecting differences. I take what I say seriously, and when I criticize something, I like to lay out a full argument. But before we act, we should understand what the policies actually say. People in China are impatient today. Before getting the facts straight, they post articles online to show how knowledgeable they are. When we read them, they are completely off the mark: what they say bears little relation to the facts.

Lee Kim Yew: I made my first fortune at twenty. Among Chinese Malaysians, once you have money, people knock on your door asking you to give to charity, mainly to support Chinese-language education. Malaysia now has 1,200 Chinese primary schools, along with secondary schools and universities that carry on Chinese culture. They all grew in part from our earlier charitable work.

When I was young, I read a line in *Vegetable Roots Discourse*: “One who does evil and fears others will know still has a path to goodness within the evil; one who does good and hastens to make it known has the root of evil within the good.” I have always followed this teaching in my charity and kept a low profile. Sometimes, after giving, people want to put your name on it. If you decline, you can seem self-consciously lofty. That creates a dilemma too.

After I began studying Buddhism at forty, I learned that giving is the first of the Six Perfections. How should we give? The highest state of giving taught in Buddhism is the purity of the three spheres: giver, recipient, and gift cannot be apprehended. Reaching that point makes us openhearted and frees us from conflict.

I came to this forum with certain hopes for “Shared Aspiration and Action, Exchange and Mutual Learning.” I believe leadership is very important to China's peaceful rise and the inheritance and renewal of Chinese culture. That is my personal view; I am not flattering anyone. India once suffered greatly, but it produced Gandhi, whom people call the “Mahatma.” In my heart, China's President Xi deserves to be called a worthy hero. Ten days ago, I attended China's “Poverty Reduction and Development Forum.” It opened my eyes: instead of talking about charity, we should discuss how truly to alleviate and reduce poverty.

I believe charity in China now involves many dilemmas, but never mind them; just choose something simple and do it. China still has sixty million people in extreme poverty, living, by the United Nations standard, on only one and a half U.S. dollars a day. Could we help alleviate and reduce such poverty? Whether we help one household or ten, how much would it cost? Yet it is not simply a matter of giving money. Their extreme poverty has historical and broader social causes. President Xi said in a speech that the work to which he had devoted the most energy as president was poverty alleviation and reduction. I believe this calls for shared aspiration and action. The greatest compassionate vow of our fundamental teacher, Shakyamuni Buddha, is that he does not want sentient beings to suffer, but wants them to leave suffering and find happiness.

Charity, especially poverty alleviation and reduction, must be led by government and jointly supported by entrepreneurs and cultural and educational organizations like ours. Religion is also a great force. But everyone should understand that even a country as rich as the United States still has people in extreme poverty. The Dharma must help us explore this question------why is life so hard?

China now has such a fine opportunity, with a worthy leader devoting his greatest energy to alleviating and reducing poverty. Poverty work requires more than money; we must get involved on the ground ourselves. We speak of exchange and mutual learning. Suppose a child from a wealthy family is pampered at home and asks his father for a phone and a car. Let him learn alongside a poor child, and he will soon be taught an important lesson. I also believe this forum can bring everyone's strength together, because sixty million is an enormous number.

I came to China very early, in 1987, when Malaysian citizens were not yet allowed to visit. I came with a deputy prime minister and spoke with a Chinese vice premier. I was young then, only thirty, and first learned that people in China had once been so poor that they starved to death. Hearing this brought tears to my eyes. Many young people today have forgotten it, but it is a fact. So we especially need “Shared Aspiration and Action, Exchange and Mutual Learning”: we should work with the government and do something practical to alleviate and reduce poverty. I greatly admire the phrase “building a community of shared life for humanity.” It echoes the Buddhist teaching that “all sentient beings are equal.”

{#四观众互动解决问题}
## IV. Audience Q&A: Addressing Practical Concerns

Yang Jinlin: Politics has entered our forum for the first time today, and the appraisal came from a Chinese Malaysian, adding another auspicious note. Let us return to our topic. From what our guests have shared, I see several traits they hold in common: humility, reverence, and equanimity.

The guests here are all from Fujian or Guangdong. Someone in the Eastern Han once said, “Fujian produces many eunuchs,” yet today great worthies and virtuous people have all appeared. This too marks a rare flourishing age for the Chinese nation. We must look to the whole world, so let us continue interacting and invite our friends and practitioners here today to engage with this subject.

Audience member: Today's subject is Buddhism and corporate culture. I would like to ask: how can we put Buddhist culture and spirit into practice in corporate culture? Sixty percent of the people in our company are Buddhists, so this concerns me deeply. I would like Master Jiqun and Mr. Cao Dewang to answer.

Master Jiqun: The distinguished lay Buddhists here all practice Buddhism in their companies. In one sense, their answers should be more convincing than mine. Mr. Cao just explained the beliefs by which he runs his business. That is in fact how he practices the Dharma------bringing its wisdom into his heart, making it his own conviction, and then carrying that conviction into his business. The next step is to enable more employees to share the same conviction and moral standards. I think our entrepreneurs should discuss the specifics.

Cao Dewang: Buddhism has different vehicles: the Human and Deva Vehicle, the Sravaka (Sound Hearer) Vehicle, the Pratyekabuddha Vehicle, and the Bodhisattva Vehicle. As businesspeople, when we are only at the level of the Human and Deva Vehicle, that is all the capacity we have. Never imagine you are already a bodhisattva. If you do and then summon others, what will happen? It is like walking past a temple and seeing people kneeling there------today is the Buddha's birthday, and that is all the level they have reached. It is nowhere near using Buddhist principles to run a business.

What does the Dharma teach? How to be a good human being. To truly become a Buddha, we must practice the Six Perfections, from giving to the final perfection of prajñā. Prajñā too has different levels. First is prajñā with attributes, wisdom that has forms and can be named. In the simplest modern terms, it means “knowing astronomy above and geography below.” If you reach that level, you will have no problem as an entrepreneur, right? Running a company by Buddhist principles is harder still! You do not have that capacity. Neither do I. I am nearly retired, and I have not yet cultivated to that level.

What, then, should we do? We cannot simply repeat hearsay or echo what others say. First, we need an honest assessment of our own level. I once read an article about Shi Potian, a character in Jin Yong's fiction. Shi Potian is a fool, an untutored hero without many ideas, but he has one special trait: he trusts everyone in the world. He believes everyone treats him well and no one means him harm, though in fact both open and hidden attacks await him. Yet he ultimately succeeds and becomes the leading figure in the martial world.

I believe what Jin Yong wrote about is right faith. For an entrepreneur, the simplest method is this------believe! Believe in the dignity of the law, in the existence of morality, that the customer is God and deserves the greatest respect, and that employees are your right and left hands. Isn't that so? To govern through Buddhism, the Buddhist tradition advocates observing precepts and giving up greed, anger, ignorance, arrogance, and doubt. Confucianism, I think, teaches the same thing under different names: benevolence, righteousness, propriety, wisdom, and trustworthiness.

Faith is therefore crucial. If you trust no one, you are certain to die. Some worry, what if I trust someone and am cheated? Actually, being cheated is your fault, not theirs, because at the very least you lack self-confidence, inner strength, and ability. That is why you were deceived. International assessments of an entrepreneur's bad record include drug use, gambling, and overdue debt. What is the last item? Whether the entrepreneur has been cheated. Even being cheated counts as a bad record; this too falls within the domain of trust.

Yang Jinlin: Being cheated is not giving, remember. It is excellent to see such enthusiasm. Come, ask questions; do not make speeches.

Chen Xingqiao: I work in the editorial department of *The Voice of Dharma* at the Buddhist Association of China. I have spent decades in Buddhist circles. The commercialization of Buddhism draws harsh criticism from ordinary people and believers alike, and those of us within the tradition feel it too. The Lingshan company is fairly well run by domestic standards. Although it is a commercial team, it has religious faith and cultural depth. Yet among domestic organizations that try to imitate it, ninety percent fail badly. A company can operate this way, of course, but if a temple did so, it might distort Buddhism. I would like Master Jiqun to discuss the commercialization of Buddhism: how can we promote healthy Buddhist cultural development while preventing commerce from damaging Buddhism's sacred resources? Many famous mountains and great temples in China have now been hijacked by business groups. Lay Buddhist Yang has done much in Buddhist circles and should understand this well. I would also like you to share your thoughts.

Master Jiqun: We must first be clear that this is not the same subject as today's “Buddhism and Corporate Culture.” Commercialization is, however, an important problem facing Buddhism today. The recent controversy over Shaolin Temple is also connected to it.

The Dharma is great wisdom for life and is vital to the inner development of Chinese people, including the formation of their worldviews, views of life, and values. Yet the negative impact of today's controversies is steadily devaluing this brand. That is not only a loss for Buddhism but for society as a whole.

If people cannot draw on this wisdom for inner development, where is the way forward for life? For society? For the country? I believe this wisdom is precisely the key to renewing the Chinese nation and realizing the Chinese Dream.

Buddhism must return to its roots and recover its basic functions: practice and spreading the Dharma. So many problems have arisen in Buddhist circles because practice is weak and Dharma teaching and education fall short. Buddhist circles themselves bear part of the responsibility, while the social environment also plays a role. The Buddhist community must therefore strengthen itself. At the same time, I hope society will recognize the value of the Dharma's wisdom and support it constructively. At the very least, people should not pass rash judgment without knowing the facts, much less create grave verbal karma. Only then can Buddhism develop soundly.

Yang Jinlin: Master Jiqun has spoken tactfully and subtly, but honestly, everyone understands. This is something all monastic and lay practitioners must face. Since Buddhism came east to this ancient land, it has gone through so many rises and falls. We all have a responsibility to face the issue.

Yang Zhao: On this question, first, every temple I support comes out ahead, because I seek nothing. Whether I help a company or a temple, whenever I give, I never expect a single yuan in return. Since my heart seeks nothing, loss is impossible.

Second, I do not give to everyone who comes to me. I must see whether a temple has the necessary conditions. So people ask, why do you sometimes give full support and at other times ignore a temple entirely? That is truly how I act.

Here are my standards. First, does it have people with genuine aspiration? My aspiration is to serve society; if you step forward to take responsibility, your aspiration too must truly be for society. Second, does it have wisdom? Aspiration without wisdom will not do. Third, does it have the right conditions? Someone may have both aspiration and wisdom, but lack blessings, so people will not support them. Fourth, I look at the place itself, chiefly whether it offers a suitable setting. If the four conditions of “Dharma, wealth, companions, and place” are present, I include it in my annual funding plan and give it full support. When we work together, it will certainly succeed. That is how I handle the relationship between business and Buddhism.

Yang Jinlin: That is very detached. Because he seeks nothing, it is rare and admirable. Who else would like to ask a question?

Audience member: So many eminent and virtuous people have gathered at today's forum; it is a rare and auspicious occasion. As a partner, we too feel deeply honored. I would like to ask Mr. Lee of Malaysia about practical implementation. It would be extremely valuable to connect the forum theme, “Shared Aspiration and Action,” concretely with “Great Path, Great Business,” and then issue an appeal to reduce poverty in China, especially extreme poverty. It also occurs to me that extremely poor families inevitably face a lack of education, which bears on cultural continuity, spreading the Dharma, and raising the next generation and those after them. Mr. Lee, what specific, practical plans do you have for the appeal you made today? If they are shared here, I believe more people will respond.

Yang Jinlin: Kim Yew, I think you can make the idea concrete. Tencent's charitable foundation has a great deal of money; if your proposal is good, it can join in.

Lee Kim Yew: To do good, we need human, financial, and material resources. In some matters, a crisis is also an opportunity. When I met Master Jiqun yesterday, I mentioned that, in business, China has learned from the West, especially over the last twenty or thirty years. There is nothing wrong with that, but I think we need something of our own. Westerners speak of the “economy,” which amounts to doing business. But in Chinese culture, I once added a character before and another after the two characters for “economy,” *jingji*, to form the phrase, “When the country prospers and the people live in peace, use the *jing* to *ji* them.” What *jing*, or “classic,” is this? Buddhist scriptures.

As we have said, many problems arise from the human heart. That is why Buddhism has the *Heart Sutra*. *Jing* also means a road: as a vehicle has a road, the *jing* is the road of our lives. And what of *ji*, “to help”? It appears in Master Jiqun's name. Once we have learned the *jing* and used it to solve many of our own problems, we must then *ji*------help the people.

I believe that hospitals, schools, and Dharma centers can generate revenue, but in these three social fields we must think of righteousness when we see gain, never forget righteousness at the sight of gain. What does righteousness mean here? Using the surplus you generate to help the poor. The West has good systems and legal standards: schools may charge fees but may not operate for private profit. Any surplus can improve the quality of teachers or school facilities, but no individual may profit from it. I believe we can learn from the West here.

As we work to alleviate poverty, I do not think money is China's problem. The problem is our hearts and methods. China now has a dangerous habit: whenever friends host a meal, eighty percent of the food is still uneaten when people leave. Malaysia too has many wealthy people, but our tradition cannot accept this. Across China, however, it happens everywhere. How can we change or regulate it? Suppose that when you spend 3,000 yuan on a meal, thirty yuan is donated automatically. That could help someone in poverty.

I am sixty and want to find a group of like-minded friends. We will train young people and help them start businesses. Once their businesses are running, they must repay the principal, which we will use to help a second young person. Their company must also give me twenty percent of its profit, which I will keep circulating. This is what I personally want to do, in answer to your question.

Yang Jinlin: Alleviating poverty is a global issue, as is reducing it. This gives us another topic to consider after the forum. Who else would like to ask a question?

Audience member: I am grateful to all our teachers and distinguished guests. My question is mainly for Master Jiqun. From what I observe, many lay Buddhists in China still understand Buddhism at the level of superstition, even misguided belief; very few possess right understanding and right faith. I have thought about this for a long time and reached a tentative conclusion. People have three outlooks------a worldview, a view of life, and values. These are confused for many Chinese people, and so their beliefs are confused too. Someone may not acknowledge samsara or the law of cause and effect at all, yet still study Buddhism. That is a contradiction. Master, please teach us how to guide these lay Buddhists onto the path of right faith.

Master Jiqun: As society develops, people pay ever more attention to spiritual and inner concerns, and more people need the Dharma. At present, the Buddhist community still does too little overall to spread the Dharma. Moreover, the Dharma is vast and profound and genuinely difficult to learn. Without guidance, most people often do not know where to begin. So I have continually explored how to help everyone study Buddhism effectively.

First, we need a progressive sequence. Education moves gradually from primary school to secondary school and university. Chinese Chan emphasizes sudden awakening, seeking to “point directly to the human mind, see one's nature, and become a Buddha.” But unless you have the sharp faculties of a superior practitioner, most people need to advance by stages.

We also need practical methods. Buddhist study now tends toward two extremes. One is too theoretical: people learn many principles but cannot apply them. The other is blind practice: people may recite scriptures and mantras every day, yet without right view they do not know how to work with the mind and simply go through the motions. We need to unite understanding and practice. That requires concrete methods for making the Dharma's wisdom our own, grounding our views of life, the world, and values in it, and then putting those views into practice in daily life and in our dealings with others.

I have now developed a “Three-Stage Practice” curriculum, which you can learn about through our website. If you follow this curriculum step by step, the sequence becomes fairly clear. With a goal, direction, method, and supportive environment, studying Buddhism becomes relatively easy.

Yang Jinlin: Because time is short, we can take only one final question.

Audience member: I am very grateful for this “Great Path, Great Business” forum. Today's theme has been excellent. I have two questions. First, what is the Great Path? Can a path be great or small? Second, from Mr. Cao Dewang's perspective, what is Great Business? Can business be great or small?

Cao Dewang: The Path is neither great nor small. Confucius asked Laozi what the Way was. Laozi replied that the Way existed before the earth took form. Whether called a great path or a small path, it is formless and everywhere. Business is neither great nor small, though enterprises do differ in size. I too started with a small company. You cut a more impressive figure now than I did when I was young.

Chinese writers like phrases that rhyme, so we have “Great Path, Great Something.” In fact, the Path is neither great nor small. It is utterly sacred and inviolable; no one can overturn it.

Master Jiqun: Let me add one sentence to Lay Buddhist Cao's remarks. The Path is neither great nor small, but because the world has so many petty paths------including gossip and rumors------we provisionally call it the “Great Path.”

Yang Jinlin: Today we discussed the “Great Path,” spreading the Dharma under its guidance, and each entrepreneur's views on success, wealth, benefit, charity, and happiness. Though this is only a beginning, I find it remarkable that we have made a beginning at all. As the forum draws to a close, I ask each speaker to summarize today's remarks in one sentence.

Yang Zhao: For an entrepreneur, where is the road? Beneath our feet.

Lee Kim Yew: Be a simple businessperson.

Cao Dewang: The true meaning of life is “faith, aspiration, and practice.”

Master Jiqun: I hope that by carrying forward traditional culture, including Buddhist and Confucian culture, we can gain a fresh understanding of success, happiness, benefit, charity, and other questions in life, redefine them, and offer wise guidance for the future development of Chinese society.

(Edited from a recording of the live forum.)
